      "problem": "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,3)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(2,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-(-1)}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+1}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of $6$ and passes through the point $(0,3).$ To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =6(x-0) \\\\ y-3 & =6x \\\\ y & =6x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
